{"path":"chrono/weekday_indexed/weekday_indexed","title":"std::chrono::weekday_indexed::weekday_indexed","titleHtml":"<span class=\"t-title-prefix\">std::chrono::weekday_indexed::</span>weekday_indexed","originalUrl":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/chrono/weekday_indexed/weekday_indexed","section":"chrono","kind":"page","summary":"A more convenient way to construct a weekday_indexed is with weekday's operator[], i.e., wd[index].","html":"<table class=\"t-dcl-begin\"><tbody>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx20\" id=\"Version_1\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">weekday_indexed<span class=\"br0\">(</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"sy1\">=</span> <span class=\"kw1\">default</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td> (1) </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx20\">(since C++20)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx20\" id=\"Version_2\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"kw4\">constexpr</span> weekday_indexed<span class=\"br0\">(</span> <span class=\"kw4\">const</span> <a href=\"/w/cpp/chrono/weekday\"><span class=\"kw2576\">std::<span class=\"me2\">chrono</span><span class=\"sy4\">::</span><span class=\"me2\">weekday</span></span></a><span class=\"sy3\">&amp;</span> wd, <span class=\"kw4\">unsigned</span> index <span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"kw1\">noexcept</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td> (2) </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx20\">(since C++20)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl-sep\"><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Constructs a <code>weekday_indexed</code>.\n</p>\n<div class=\"t-li1\"><span class=\"t-li\">1)</span> Default constructor leaves both the <span class=\"t-lc\">std::chrono::weekday</span> and the index value uninitialized.</div>\n<div class=\"t-li1\"><span class=\"t-li\">2)</span> Constructs a <span class=\"t-lc\">weekday_indexed</span> storing the weekday <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">wd</span></code> and the index <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">index</span></code>. The values held are unspecified if <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"sy3\">!</span>wd.<span class=\"me1\">ok</span><span class=\"br0\">(</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"sy3\">||</span> index <span class=\"sy1\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"nu0\">7</span></span></code>.</div>\n<h3 id=\"Notes\">Notes</h3>\n<p>A more convenient way to construct a <span class=\"t-lc\">weekday_indexed</span> is with <span class=\"t-lc\">weekday</span>'s <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">operator<span class=\"br0\">[</span><span class=\"br0\">]</span></span></code>, i.e., <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">wd<span class=\"br0\">[</span>index<span class=\"br0\">]</span></span></code>.\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"Example\">Example</h3>\n<div class=\"t-example\">\n<pre data-lang=\"cpp\"><code><span class=\"co2\">#include &lt;chrono&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"co2\">#include &lt;iostream&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"kw1\">using</span> <span class=\"kw1\">namespace</span> std<span class=\"sy4\">::</span><span class=\"me2\">chrono</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span>\n&nbsp;\n<span class=\"kw4\">int</span> main<span class=\"br0\">(</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span>\n<span class=\"br0\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"kw4\">constexpr</span> <span class=\"kw4\">auto</span> third_friday <span class=\"sy1\">=</span> weekday_indexed<span class=\"br0\">(</span>Friday, <span class=\"nu0\">3</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span> <span class=\"co1\">// uses constructor (2)</span>\n    static_assert<span class=\"br0\">(</span>third_friday <span class=\"sy1\">==</span> Friday<span class=\"br0\">[</span><span class=\"nu0\">3</span><span class=\"br0\">]</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span>\n&nbsp;\n    weekday_indexed wdi <span class=\"sy1\">=</span> Tuesday<span class=\"br0\">[</span><span class=\"nu0\">2</span><span class=\"br0\">]</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span> <span class=\"co1\">// represents the 2nd Tuesday</span>\n    <a href=\"/w/cpp/io/cout\"><span class=\"kw1775\">std::<span class=\"me2\">cout</span></span></a> <span class=\"sy1\">&lt;&lt;</span> year_month_day<span class=\"br0\">{</span> wdi <span class=\"sy2\">/</span> October <span class=\"sy2\">/</span> 2019y <span class=\"br0\">}</span> <span class=\"sy1\">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class=\"st0\">'<span class=\"es1\">\\n</span>'</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span>\n<span class=\"br0\">}</span></code></pre>\n<p>Possible output:\n</p>\n<pre data-output=\"1\"><code>2019-10-08</code></pre> \n</div>\n<h3 id=\"See_also\">See also</h3>\n<table class=\"t-dsc-begin\">\n<tr class=\"t-dsc\">\n<td>  <div class=\"t-dsc-member-div\"><div><a href=\"/w/cpp/chrono/weekday/operator_at\"> <span class=\"t-lines\"><span>operator[]</span></span></a></div></div>\n</td>\n<td>  convenience syntax for constructing a <a href=\"/w/cpp/chrono/weekday_indexed\"><code>weekday_indexed</code></a> or <a href=\"/w/cpp/chrono/weekday_last\"><code>weekday_last</code></a> from this <code>weekday</code> <br> <span class=\"t-mark\">(public member function of <code>std::chrono::weekday</code>)</span> </td></tr>\n</table>","headings":[{"depth":3,"text":"Notes","id":"Notes"},{"depth":3,"text":"Example","id":"Example"},{"depth":3,"text":"See also","id":"See_also"}],"breadcrumbs":[{"path":"","title":"C++ reference"},{"path":"chrono","title":"Date and time utilities"},{"path":"chrono/weekday_indexed","title":"std::chrono::weekday_indexed"}],"children":[],"map":{"label":"std::chrono::weekday_indexed::weekday_indexed","children":[{"label":"Declarations","note":"2 declarations; revisions: since C++20. Constructs a weekday_indexed."},{"label":"Notes","note":"A more convenient way to construct a weekday_indexed is with weekday's operator[], i.e., wd[index]."},{"label":"Example"